   "wikipedia": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Everyone_Is_Everybody_Else",
   "resumo_en": "Everyone Is Everybody Else is the fifth studio album by British rock band Barclay James Harvest released in June 1974. This was their first album for the Polydor label after they had parted company with EMI.\nThe album was produced by Rodger Bain, who had previously worked with Black Sabbath in producing their first three albums. He also produced heavy rock bands Judas Priest and Budgie. There was said to be strained relations between Bain and the band, due to the preference of his musical style, with the band unhappy with the results of the song \"Child of the Universe\" in particular; Woolly Wolstenholme's only contribution was also left off the original album.\nThe album was played extensively on Radio Caroline, particularly the tracks \"For No One\" (whose lyrics contain the title of the album) and \"Child of the Universe\". The album was voted by Radio Caroline listeners at No. 13 on  the Top 100 All Time Album Chart. The album led to a BBC Radio 1 session with John Peel.",
